1":"to sojourn","H8033":"there","H4616":"because","H7045":"curse","H2781":"reproach","H1471":"nation","H7911":"to forget","H4428":"king","H1792":"to crush","H5704":"till","H3372":"to fear","H1980":"went","H8451":"instruction","H2708":"statute","H5414":"give","H3651":"right","H7760":"make","H3947":"take","H8552":"to finish","H5307":"fall","H2719":"sword","H7458":"famine","H6996":"small","H4191":"to die","H423":"oath","H8047":"destroyed","H6485":"list","H1698":"pestilence","H6412":"survivor","H8300":"survivor","H5375":"raise","H3588":"for","H518":"if","H6030":"to dwell","H5975":"stand","H6951":"assembly","H5971":"people","H1696":"speak","H8034":"name","H3318":"come","H6310":"lip","H4446":"queen","H8064":"heaven","H5258":"to pour","H5262":"drink offering","H587":"we","H8269":"ruler","H7646":"to satisfy","H3899":"food","H2896":"pleasant","H4480":"from","H227":"then","H2308":"to cease","H2637":"to lack","H1107":"beside","H3561":"bun","H6087":"to hurt","H1397":"great man","H7002":"incense","H2142":"to remember","H5927":"rise","H3820":"heart","H3201":"be able","H5750":"still","H7455":"evil","H4611":"deed","H2398":"to sin","H6963":"voice","H5715":"testimony","H7122":"meet","H4390":"to fill","H5088":"vow","H5087":"to vow","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H7650":"to swear","H7121":"call to","H2416":"alive","H136":"Lord","H3069":"YHWH/God","H8245":"to watch","H3615":"finish","H4962":"man","H4557":"number","H4310":"who?","H226":"miraculous","H5002":"utterance","H589":"I","H4725":"place","H6548":"(Pharaoh)-hophra","H341":"enemy","H1245":"to seek","H6667":"Zedekiah","H5019":"Nebuchadnezzar","H894":"Babylon"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",17,null,"challoner","The queen of heaven.... The moon, which they worshipped under this name."],["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Magdal, the third station of the Israelites, (Ex. xiv. 2.) or another \"tower\" twelve miles from Pelusium. C. xlvi. 14. — Memphis, the capital of Nome, near the western banks of the Nile, and famous for its pyramids, which are standing, though the town be no more. C. xlvi. 19. — Phatures; perhaps in higher Egypt. Jeremias might go or send to these cities, this his last prediction, (Calmet) to reclaim his countrymen from idolatry. (Haydock)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Inhabitant: or the number is exceedingly small. (Calmet) — All the Jews had retired. (Haydock) — It would be well if people would reflect, how those who have given way to heresy and infidelity have been treated. (Worthington)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Forth, drop by drop. C. xlii. 18. I have repeatedly warned them by punishments. (Haydock)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Gods. Their blindness and malice was inconceivable. They attribute their past miseries to the neglect of worshipping the moon! v. 18. They may be compared to a mixture of iron and brass, which is put through the crucible in vain. C. vi. 29. Ezec. xxii. 18."],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Face, to punish grievously. Ezec. iv. 3. and xx. 35. (Calmet) — All did not perish, but the far greatest part. v. 14. 28. (Worthington)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Desire and expectation. C. xxii. 27. They meant to return as soon as the Chaldees had left the country: but their hose were vain. The enemy would fall upon Egypt, after he had taken Tyre and the neighbouring provinces; and those who had not abandoned that country in time, would perish. v. 12. 26. (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","The queen of heaven; the moon, which they worshipped under this name, (Challoner) as also under that of the Celestial Venus, (Theodoret) Diana, and Isis. It seems the Jews had engaged by vow (v. 27.) to restore her worship. (Calmet) — The women more particularly adored the moon: yet all were guilty. C. vii. 18. and 4 K. xxiii. 5. (Worthington)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Famine. They think nothing of the transgression of the law, &c. v. 23. Could obduracy and blindness go greater lengths? 1 Mac. i. 12."],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Worship. Hebrew \"to represent.\" The cakes had a crescent on them. C. See Hesychius in selenai. — Husbands. The women are most zealous. But all partook of this idolatry. C. vii. 18. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Heart, or mind to punish you for idolatry. Sacrifices could be offered to the Lord only in his temple. (Haydock)"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Liveth. They swore by his name as by that of idols. God could not abide such company. (Calmet) — Not one of these obstinate Jews shall be spared. v. 14. Others, like Jeremias, the Septuagint and the holy family, no doubt called upon the Lord in the land of Egypt. (Haydock)"],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Enemies. Herodotus (ii. 161. 169.) informs us that Apries reigned fortunately twenty-five years, when he lost many of his men fighting against the Cyreneans, and was dethroned by Amasis, and strangled by his subjects. Usher (A. 3433) suspects that Amasis was assisted and confirmed by Nabuchodonosor, and he might this fulfill the prophecies. C. xliii. 11. &c. Ezec. xxix. &c. Josephus (Ant. x. 11.) and Berosus (c. Ap. i.) assert, that Nabuchodonosor defeated and slew the king of Egypt, though Herodotus is silent on this subject, his account being communicated by the Egyptian priests, and full of fables. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[8,10,0,"Homilies on Romans","Homily 14",0,407,"… But when they kept not up to this, but deserted to Egypt, this indeed He allowed them, but requires of them not to desert to irreligion as well as to Egypt. But when they did not comply in this either, He sendeth the prophet along with them, so that they might not after all suffer total wreck. For since they did not follow Him when He called, He next followeth them to discipline them, and hinder their being hurried further into vice, and as a father full of affection does a child who takes all treatment in the same peevish way, conducting him about everywhere with himself, and following him about. …"]],"fau":[["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 11 (1889)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf111"]]}